invariant: INV-16
file: p256_wide_inverse.c
catches: p256_equiv_test
reason: combine_modular no longer gives u and v the sign a and b took, so a
  round that negates a below zero leaves a = -u y modulo m where the rounds
  rest on a = u y. Most inverses then come out wrong, and
  bin/p256_equiv_test holds both inverses to the 32-bit files' Fermat
  inverses.

--- old
    f_negative ^= negate;
    g_negative ^= negate;
--- new
    (void)negate;
